---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const corpfinCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · The financing decision', note: 'Fund projects with debt (cheap, tax-deductible interest, but obligations) or equity (no obligations, but dilution and costlier). The mix is capital structure.' },
    { math: '2 · WACC: the hurdle rate', note: 'Weighted Average Cost of Capital = blend of debt and equity costs, weighted by their shares. The minimum return a project must clear — the discount rate from the NPV lesson, finally sourced.' },
    { math: '3 · Modigliani–Miller: the irrelevance baseline', note: 'In a perfect world (no taxes, no bankruptcy costs), capital structure is IRRELEVANT — the pie is the same size however sliced (Nobel 1985/1990). Everything real comes from the frictions they assumed away.' },
    { math: '4 · Real frictions drive real choices', note: 'Tax shield favours debt; bankruptcy costs and financial distress favour equity; agency costs (managers hoarding cash vs over-borrowing) pull both ways. Observed structures are the equilibrium of these forces.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/finance/advanced/corporate-finance">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Corporate finance answers <strong>three questions for a firm: which projects to invest in
    (NPV), how to fund them (debt vs equity), and what to return to owners (dividends vs
    buybacks)</strong>. The framework is the NPV lesson plus a capital-structure theory — with
    Modigliani–Miller proving that in a frictionless world none of it matters, and real frictions
    proving that in ours, all of it does.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="capital structure, WACC, and the irrelevance theorem that organises everything" widget="equationStep" config={corpfinCfg} />

  <h2>Decisions in practice</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Project selection:</strong> NPV positive → invest; mutually exclusive → highest NPV; capital-rationed → profitability index ranking. Simple rules, honest inputs (the hard part).</li>
    <li><strong>Dividends vs buybacks:</strong> economically similar at fair prices; buybacks are flexible and tax-efficient in many systems, dividends are commitments. Payout policy signals management's confidence about future cash.</li>
    <li><strong>M&A and valuation:</strong> acquisitions price companies by discounted cash flows and multiples — and most destroy acquirer value (synergy optimism is the recurring villain). The evidence is sobering and well documented.</li>
    <li><strong>Working capital:</strong> the least glamorous decision — receivables, inventory, payables timing — is where cash-flow crises actually happen (the business ladder's operations lesson connects).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Modigliani & Miller (1958); Brealey, Myers & Allen; Graham & Harvey, "The Theory and Practice of Corporate Finance" (2001).</p>
</TopicPage>
